 ### Brick Active Check
-Checks if a brick is currently active in the world tile occupancy mask to filter out stale or unloaded bricks in ray-tracing queries [S4, unofficial]. It calculates brick coordinates $(bx, by, bz)$ from a linear brick index $bi$ using modulo and division operations relative to `WORLD_BRICKS_X` and `WORLD_BRICKS_Y` [S4, unofficial]. It then maps these to a tile index $ti$ using chunking (`>> 2`) and a world tile index function [S4, unofficial]. Finally, it computes a linear offset `lin` within the tile using bitwise AND operations and returns the result of `tile_has_child(ti, lin)` [S4, unofficial]. This method is specific to world-space brick management rather than pure grid hierarchy traversal [S4, unofficial].
+Checks if a brick is currently active in the world tile occupancy mask to filter out stale or unloaded bricks in ray-tracing queries [S4, unofficial]. It calculates brick coordinates $(bx, by, bz)$ from a linear brick index $bi$ using `bx = bi % WORLD_BRICKS_X`, `by = (bi / WORLD_BRICKS_X) % WORLD_BRICKS_Y`, and `bz = bi / (WORLD_BRICKS_X * WORLD_BRICKS_Y)` [S4, unofficial]. It then maps these to a tile index $ti$ using `ti = world_tile_idx(bx >> 2, by >> 2, bz >> 2)` [S4, unofficial]. Finally, it computes a linear offset `lin = (bx & 3) + (bz & 3) * 4 + (by & 3) * 16` within the tile and returns `tile_has_child(ti, lin)` [S4, unofficial]. This method is specific to world-space brick management rather than pure grid hierarchy traversal [S4, unofficial].
 
